---
title: Defining an event handler
description: Writing subscribers, how many run, what happens when one throws, and how publishing differs from sending.
---

An event handler — a subscriber — reacts to something that already happened. Every matching one
runs, and none of them answers the publisher.

## Writing one

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class SendShipmentEmail(IEmailSender sender) : IEventHandler<OrderShipped>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(OrderShipped @event, ErgosfareContext context)
        => sender.SendAsync(@event.CustomerEmail, "Your order shipped", context.CancellationToken);
}
```

`IEventHandler<TEvent>` returns `ValueTask` and nothing else — a broadcast has no result to
produce.

## Many subscribers, one event

```csharp
public sealed class SendShipmentEmail  : IEventHandler<OrderShipped> { … }
public sealed class UpdateReadModel    : IEventHandler<OrderShipped> { … }
public sealed class AuditOrderEvents   : IEventHandler<OrderEvent>   { … }
```

Publishing an `OrderShipped` runs all three. Unlike commands, this is not a contest — `ERGO010`
does not apply to events, because delivering to everyone is the point.

They run in `[Weight]` order, direct registrations before covariant ones. Ordering exists, but
relying on it between subscribers is a smell: if B must run after A, they are probably one
operation.

## When one throws

The exception propagates out of the publish. Subscribers that had already run stay run;
subscribers after it do not.

That is rarely what you want, so a subscriber that can fail should contain its own failure:

```csharp
public sealed class SendShipmentEmail(IEmailSender sender, ILogger<SendShipmentEmail> logger)
    : IEventHandler<OrderShipped>
{
    public async ValueTask HandleAsync(OrderShipped @event, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        try
        {
            await sender.SendAsync(@event.CustomerEmail, "Your order shipped", context.CancellationToken);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            logger.LogError(ex, "shipment email failed for {Order}", @event.OrderId);
        }
    }
}
```

An exception interceptor on the event pipeline can do this centrally instead. Either way, decide
deliberately — the default is that one failing subscriber stops the rest.

## Publishing

```csharp
await mediator.PublishAsync(new OrderShipped(id, email, at));
await mediator.PublishAsync(@event, ct);
await mediator.PublishAsync(@event, GroupSet.Of("audit"), ct);
await mediator.PublishAsync(@event, context);          // share the dispatching context
```

A publish returns when every subscriber has completed. It is not fire-and-forget: if you want
that, put the work on a queue and let a consumer dispatch it.

{% aside type="note" %}
Reaching nobody is legal and returns. There is no flag to make it throw — that condition is
`ERGO005` at build time instead, so the case a flag protected you from is caught earlier.
{% /aside %}

## Publishing from a handler

Common, and the shape worth getting right:

```csharp
public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler(IOrderRepository repository, IEventMediator events)
    : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder, Guid>
{
    public async ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var order = Order.Create(command.CustomerId, command.Lines);
        await repository.AddAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);

        await events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(order.Id), context);
        return order.Id;
    }
}
```

Passing `context` keeps the publish in the same logical operation — same items, same
cancellation.

**Watch the transaction boundary.** Publishing before a commit lets a subscriber act on state
that later rolls back. Collect the events and publish after committing — see the
[Unit of work recipe](/ergosfare.docs/preview/recipes/unit-of-work).

## Subscribers get the full pipeline

Interceptors apply to events like anything else: `IEventPreInterceptor`,
`IEventPostInterceptor`, `IEventExceptionInterceptor`, `IEventFinalInterceptor`.

The pre and post seams name the boundary around a *delivery*, so in a broadcast they run once
per subscriber rather than once per publish.

## Registering

Nothing to write — `RegisterGenerated()` finds subscribers like any other participant:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .AddEventModule(module => module.RegisterGenerated()));
```

## Testing

```csharp
var sender = new RecordingEmailSender();
var handler = new SendShipmentEmail(sender);

await handler.HandleAsync(new OrderShipped(id, "a@b.c", DateTimeOffset.UtcNow),
                          new ErgosfareContext());

Assert.Single(sender.Sent);
```

To test that *all* the subscribers you expect run, build a container and publish — that is the
part a unit test cannot tell you.
